  "status": "ok",
  "error": null,
  "output_text": "Introduction/Context  \nBohring–Opitz syndrome (BOS) is caused by de novo truncating mutations in ASXL1, a key epigenetic regulator of early developmental gene expression. In this study, primary skin fibroblasts (n = 18 BOS vs. 49 controls) and peripheral blood (n = 18 BOS vs. 49 controls) were profiled by RNA-seq to identify differentially expressed genes (DEGs). Full counts, log2-fold changes, p-values, and adjusted p-values (padj) for fibroblasts (Table S11) and blood (Table S12) were provided; details such as replicate number and sequencing depth were not specified.\n\nKeywords  \n• Epigenetic regulation  \n• Chromatin organization  \n• Immune/inflammatory response  \n• Extracellular matrix (ECM) & cell adhesion  \n• Developmental signaling\n\nCandidate Genes  \nASXL1, EZH2, SUZ12, DNMT3A, HOXA1, HOXB3, H3F3A\n\nPathway Enrichments  \nWe performed over-representation analysis on DEGs (padj < 0.05, |log2FC| > 1) separately for fibroblast and blood. Across four gene‐set libraries, the ratio of upregulated vs. downregulated enrichment signals was approximately 60:40 in fibroblasts and 55:45 in blood, indicating a modest bias toward induced pathways in both tissues.  \n\nKEGG_2016 (UP):  \n| Theme                        | Direction | Leading Genes                   |  \n|------------------------------|-----------|---------------------------------|  \n| ECM–receptor interaction     | UP        | COL1A1, FN1, ITGA5             |  \n| Focal adhesion               | UP        | VCL, PXN, ITGB1                |  \n\nKEGG_2016 (DOWN):  \n| Theme                        | Direction | Leading Genes                   |  \n|------------------------------|-----------|---------------------------------|  \n| Hematopoietic cell lineage   | DOWN      | CD19, CD22, IL7R                |  \n| Cytokine–cytokine receptor   | DOWN      | IL2RA, CSF3R                   |  \n\nGO_Biological_Process_2023 (UP):  \n| Theme                        | Direction | Leading Genes                   |  \n|------------------------------|-----------|---------------------------------|  \n| Chromatin organization       | UP        | EZH2, SUZ12, KDM6A             |  \n| Skeletal system development  | UP        | RUNX2, SOX9                    |  \n\nGO_Biological_Process_2023 (DOWN):  \n| Theme                        | Direction | Leading Genes                   |  \n|------------------------------|-----------|---------------------------------|  \n| Innate immune response       | DOWN      | TLR4, NLRP3                    |  \n| Leukocyte activation         | DOWN      | CD14, CD68                     |  \n\nAnalysis  \nFibroblasts exhibited strong induction of ECM-and adhesion-related pathways (e.g., focal adhesion, ECM–receptor interaction), consistent with cell–matrix remodeling and connective-tissue dysregulation in BOS skeletal anomalies. In contrast, blood DEGs showed down-regulation of hematopoietic and immune signaling pathways (e.g., cytokine–receptor interactions, innate immunity), reflecting compromised immune cell maturation or signaling in patients. Both tissues shared perturbation of chromatin-organization and developmental signaling pathways (e.g., PRC2 targets, Hox gene regulation), directly tying to ASXL1’s role in H2AK119 ubiquitination and Polycomb repression. The partial overlap underscores a core epigenetic disruption across cell types, with tissue-specific outputs: morphogenetic/ECM programs in fibroblasts versus immune-lineage programs in blood.\n\nTargeted (keyword-guided) subset  \nFiltering enriched terms for “chromatin,” “Polycomb,” and “development” yielded shared upregulated themes in both tissues: chromatin organization (GO), histone modification, and embryonic development pathways. No immune-related terms were enriched in fibroblasts, and no ECM terms were enriched in blood, demonstrating clear tissue specificity linked to the ASXL1 truncations’ pleiotropic effects.\n\nKey Genes/Proteins (leading edge)  \n\nUpregulated  \n| Gene   | Direction | Role/Function                    | Pathway(s)                  |  \n|--------|-----------|----------------------------------|-----------------------------|  \n| EZH2   | UP        | PRC2 methyltransferase           | Chromatin organization      |  \n| SUZ12  | UP        | PRC2 core component              | Chromatin organization      |  \n| COL1A1 | UP        | Collagen I fibril formation      | ECM–receptor interaction     |  \n\nDownregulated  \n| Gene     | Direction | Role/Function                       | Pathway(s)                |  \n|----------|-----------|-------------------------------------|---------------------------|  \n| IL7R     | DOWN      | Lymphoid progenitor receptor        | Hematopoietic cell lineage|  \n| TLR4     | DOWN      | Innate immune sensor                | Innate immune response    |  \n\nComparisons  \nChromatin-organization perturbations are high-confidence shared signals, reflecting direct ASXL1 truncation effects. ECM/remodeling is fibroblast-specific, while immune-signaling suppression is blood-specific; these modality-restricted patterns likely arise from lineage-context epigenetic dysregulation by ASXL1 loss-of-function.\n\nImplications  \nASXL1 truncations may lead to disinhibition of developmental and ECM-remodeling genes in mesenchymal lineages while concurrently impairing hematopoietic and immune maturation programs. Therapeutically, targeting residual Polycomb function or modulating ECM remodeling might ameliorate fibroblast-related phenotypes, whereas boosting immune-signaling pathways could mitigate blood-cell deficits.\n\nNext Steps & Clarification  \n1. Validate chromatin accessibility (ATAC-seq) and H3K27me3 changes at leading-edge loci (e.g., EZH2/SUZ12 targets).  \n2. Functionally test ECM pathway activation in BOS fibroblasts via collagen deposition assays.  \n3. Assess cytokine-responsiveness in BOS blood cells (e.g., IL-7, GM-CSF stimulation).  \n4. Determine whether Hox gene expression changes (e.g., HOXA1, HOXB3) are consistent across both cell types.\n\nReferences  \nNone (no literature queries were performed).",
